edits.newText:
### limit.onIdle(options?)

Returns a promise that resolves when the limiter is idle.

The limiter is idle when there are no pending promises waiting to run and no promises currently running (including slots that have been reserved but whose function has not started yet).

Calling this when the limiter is already idle resolves immediately. Multiple callers can await the same idle state, and the promise resolves even when the running or queued promises reject.

```js
const limit = pLimit(1);

limit(() => doSomething());
limit(() => doSomethingElse());

await limit.onIdle();
// All queued and running work has settled.
```

#### options

Type: `object`

#### signal

Type: `AbortSignal`

An `AbortSignal` that rejects the returned promise with `signal.reason` when aborted.

Aborting only rejects this particular waiter. It never cancels running or queued jobs, and it never rejects other waiters. If the signal is already aborted, the promise rejects immediately with `signal.reason`, even when the limiter is already idle.

The abort listener is removed once the promise settles.

```js
const limit = pLimit(1);
const controller = new AbortController();

limit(() => doSomething());

try {
	await limit.onIdle({signal: controller.signal});
} catch (error) {
	// `error` is `controller.signal.reason`.
}

controller.abort(new Error('Aborted'));
```

